course: biology
lesson: 277
title: DNA simply: the recipe in every cell
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Almost every cell in your body carries a copy of the same long recipe book, called DNA, and it is shaped like a twisted ladder.
explain: The recipe is written with only four chemical letters, A, T, C and G, and the order of the rungs tells the cell how to build and run a body, just as the order of letters in a cake recipe tells you what to bake.
explain: Stretched out, the DNA from one tiny cell would be about two metres long, so it is wound up very tightly, like a garden hose coiled into a matchbox.
explain: You got half your DNA from your mum and half from your dad, mixed in a new way, which is why you can have your mum's eyes and your dad's ears while your sister looks different.
explain: Small copying mistakes in DNA, called mutations, are how living things slowly change, so DNA is both the recipe of one body and the long history of life.
fact: DNA is a long molecule shaped like a twisted ladder, found in almost every cell; the order of its rungs is a recipe for building and running a body.
fact: DNA uses only four chemical letters: A, T, C and G.
fact: Stretched out, the DNA in one human cell is about two metres long, packed tightly into the tiny nucleus; all the DNA in a body would reach the Sun and back many times.
fact: You get half your DNA from your mother and half from your father; which parts you get is a mix, so brothers and sisters differ, but identical twins share the same DNA.
fact: Many basic recipes in cells, such as how to use energy, are shared by all life, so a person shares many genes with a banana in a broad sense; the numbers you hear are rough and often misused.
fact: People share about 98 percent of their DNA with chimpanzees, our closest living relatives; we had a common ancestor about 6 to 7 million years ago, and small differences in the recipe make big differences in body and brain.
fact: Everyone except identical twins has slightly different DNA, so a sample from hair roots, skin or spit can identify a person and show who is related to whom.
fact: Neanderthals were close human relatives who died out long ago; scientists have read DNA from Neanderthal bones tens of thousands of years old and found that many people today carry a small part of Neanderthal DNA; Svante Pääbo won a Nobel Prize for this in 2022.
fact: Changes in DNA are called mutations; they happen when cells copy DNA with small mistakes, and most do nothing, some are harmful and a few are useful.
fact: Mutations are the raw material of evolution.
fact: Animals shed skin and slime into water, so testing a cup of lake water can show which fish, frogs or rare animals live there; this is called environmental DNA.
fact: Dog DNA tests compare a dog's DNA with many breeds and can show a mix of breeds; all dog breeds carry DNA from wolves.
fact: DNA studies suggest there may be four species of giraffe, not one, which matters for protecting each kind; scientists still debate how many there are.
exercise: What shape is DNA? | A twisted ladder | A round ball | A flat square | A long straight stick
exercise: How many chemical letters does DNA use? | Four | Two | Twenty-six | One hundred
exercise: Which letters are used in DNA? | A, T, C and G | A, B, C and D | X, Y and Z | Only 0 and 1
exercise: What does the order of the rungs in DNA do? | It is a recipe for building and running a body | It decides what the weather will be like in your town | It shows exactly how old you are | It holds your memories of holidays and birthdays
exercise: About how long is the DNA in one human cell, stretched out? | About two metres | About two millimetres | About two kilometres | About as long as a hair is thick
exercise: How much of your DNA comes from your dad? | About half | All of it | None of it | Only one letter
exercise: Who share exactly the same DNA? | Identical twins | Any brother and sister | A mum and her baby | Best friends who live together
exercise: Why do people share some DNA with bananas? | All life shares basic recipes, like how to use energy | People evolved from bananas a very, very long time ago | Bananas copy DNA from the people who eat them | Bananas are really a strange kind of animal
exercise: About how much of our DNA do we share with chimpanzees? | About 98 percent | About 10 percent | Exactly 50 percent | None of it
exercise: How long ago did people and chimps share a common ancestor? | About 6 to 7 million years ago | About 600 years ago | About 66 million years ago | About 3.5 billion years ago
exercise: Which part of a hair can give police a person's DNA? | The root | The tip | The colour | The curl
exercise: What did Svante Pääbo find? | Many people today carry a little Neanderthal DNA | Dinosaur DNA inside the mosquitoes kept in old amber | The very first DNA ever found in bananas | That chimps have no DNA at all in their cells
exercise: What is a mutation? | A change in DNA from a small copying mistake | A new kind of mobile phone | A cut on the skin that heals and leaves a scar | A cold caught from a friend
exercise: What do most mutations do? | Nothing at all | Give people superpowers | Turn people into animals | Always make people ill
exercise: What is environmental DNA? | Traces of DNA animals leave in water or soil | DNA that only plants have, deep inside their leaves | DNA made from nothing in a laboratory | DNA that falls to Earth on space rocks
exercise: What can a dog DNA test show? | The mix of breeds in a dog | How many tricks the dog knows | The dog's favourite food | How fast the dog can run
exercise: What did DNA studies suggest about giraffes? | There may be four species, not one | Giraffes are a kind of horse | All giraffes are identical twins | Giraffes have no DNA in their necks
test: «I've got mum's nose and dad's ears, so I'm like 50/50» What is closest to the truth? | Yes, you got half your DNA from each parent, mixed in a new way | No, you got all of your DNA from your mum, and only your name from your dad | No, your DNA comes from your grandparents only | No, DNA has nothing to do with looks
test: «so we're like 60 percent banana lol, I'm basically a fruit» What is right? | No, we share some basic cell recipes with bananas, and those numbers are often misused | Yes, people really are part fruit, and that is exactly why we like eating bananas so much | Yes, and bananas are part human | No, we share nothing with plants
test: «98 percent chimp?? so I'm basically a chimp» What is right? | No, small differences in the recipe make big differences in body and brain | Yes, people and chimps are the same species, just with different hair and clothes | Yes, chimps can do everything people can | No, we share only 2 percent with chimps
test: On a TV crime show, the detective finds one hair. What must the hair have to give useful DNA? | Its root, with cells in it | A bright colour | A long length | A curl at the end
test: What would happen if one scientist tested a cup of pond water and found newt DNA? | It would show a newt lives in the pond, even if nobody saw it | It would mean the water is poisonous and nobody should go near the pond | It would mean a newt was drunk by mistake | It would show there are no fish there
test: In Tom and Jerry, Jerry's little nephew Nibbles looks just like a tiny copy of Jerry. Why do real relatives usually look a bit different? | They share only some of their DNA, mixed in different ways | Relatives have no DNA in common, only the same surname | Only twins can share any DNA | Real relatives always look exactly alike
test: «mutation means you turn into a ninja turtle right» What is right? | No, a mutation is a small change in DNA, and most do nothing | Yes, mutations give animals superpowers and make them talk | Yes, but only in sewers | No, mutations happen only to plants
test: Count them: 3 pairs of identical twins come to a party. How many people are there, and how many different DNA recipes? | 6 people and 3 different DNA recipes | 6 people and 6 different DNA recipes | 3 people and 3 DNA recipes | 6 people and 1 DNA recipe
test: «if my DNA's 2 metres in every cell, why ain't I massive» What is the answer? | It is coiled up very tightly into the tiny nucleus | It is stretched outside your body in an invisible cloud | The DNA is in your bones only | It shrinks every night when you sleep
test: «Neanderthals died out, so nobody's got their DNA, duh» What is right? | Many people today carry a small part of Neanderthal DNA | Only Neanderthal fossils carry it, buried in caves in Europe | Nobody has any, they were not related to us | Everyone today is half Neanderthal
test: «sausage dog and a husky, totally different, can't both come from wolves» What is right? | All dog breeds carry DNA from wolves | Only huskies come from wolves | Sausage dogs come from badgers | Dogs come from foxes, not wolves
exam: Identify the four chemical letters employed in the genetic code of DNA. | A, T, C and G | A, B, C and D | X, Y, Z and W | R, S, T and U
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the inheritance of DNA by a child. | Approximately one half is derived from each parent | The entirety is derived from the mother | The entirety is derived from the father | It is acquired from the environment in the years following birth
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the majority of mutations? | They exert no observable effect upon the organism | They invariably confer extraordinary abilities | They invariably prove fatal | They occur exclusively in plants
exam: State the term employed for traces of genetic material shed by organisms into water or soil. | Environmental DNA | Fossil DNA | Artificial DNA | Mineral DNA
